<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adolf Hitler</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1889">1889</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adolf Hitler was the leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> and dictator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1933 to 1945. Appointed chancellor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> in January 1933, he quickly abolished democracy and built a totalitarian state. His expansionist wars began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1939, and his regime carried out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>. He killed himself in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in April 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler.jpg" alt="Adolf Hitler" width="320" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heinrich Hoffmann · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitler_portrait_crop_(cropped)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Adolf Hitler was born on 20 April 1889 in Braunau am Inn, a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> close to the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. He was the fourth of six children of Alois Hitler and his third wife, Klara Pölzl, and three siblings died in infancy. Alois was the illegitimate child of Maria Schicklgruber and bore her surname until 1876, when he took the name Hitler. Adolf's refusal to accept school discipline led to conflict with his father, who beat him; his mother tried to protect him. He moved to Germany in 1913.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In July 1919 Hitler was sent by the army to infiltrate the German Workers' Party (DAP), which he joined as member 555, the party having begun its count at 500 to seem larger. The party became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a>, and in 1921 he was appointed its leader. A coup attempt at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> in 1923 failed; he was sentenced to five years in prison, served just over a year, and dictated the first volume of Mein Kampf there.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> gave the party its opening: it won 107 seats in 1930, and after the elections of November 1932 it held the most seats in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> without a majority. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-von-papen">Franz von Papen</a> and other conservatives persuaded President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a> to appoint Hitler chancellor on 30 January 1933. The Enabling Act of 23 March began the change to Nazi Germany, and after Hindenburg died on 2 August 1934 Hitler became head of state. Unemployment fell from six million in 1932 to fewer than one million in 1936. He ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuhrerprinzip">Führerprinzip</a>, giving contradictory orders to set subordinates against one another.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> on 1 September 1939, and Hitler ordered the invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in June 1941. By January 1942 he had decided that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> and other deportees considered undesirable should be killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 29 April 1945 Hitler married Eva Braun in the Führerbunker in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, and the next day they killed themselves to avoid capture by the Soviet Red Army. Few Germans mourned him. The historian Ian Kershaw calls him the embodiment of modern political evil.</p><p>According to R. J. Rummel, the Nazi regime was responsible for the killing of an estimated 19.3 million civilians and prisoners of war, in addition to about six million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> murdered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>. A further 28.7 million soldiers and civilians died from military action in the European theatre. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> suffered wholesale destruction, described as Stunde Null, or Zero Hour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Adolf Hitler” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its greatest political export was Fascism: Mussolini’s regime, with its mass party, leader cult and corporatist claims, became a template for movements across Europe and influenced <b>Adolf Hitler</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Adolf Hitler</b> ordered the destruction of Yugoslavia, and on 6 April 1941 German aircraft bombed Belgrade, killing thousands, while German, Italian and Hungarian forces invaded.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hitler</b> never formally abolished the Weimar constitution: the Enabling Act of 1933 was renewed by the Reichstag in 1937 and 1939 and extended by decree in 1943.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The name &quot;Weimar Republic&quot; was first used by <b>Adolf Hitler</b> in 1929; officially the state kept the name German Reich, and the term only became mainstream in the 1950s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…had established personal and totalitarian rule by 1929; Benito Mussolini came to power in Italy in 1922 and from 1925 built the first fascist dictatorship; and <b>Adolf Hitler</b>, appointed chancellor in January 1933, used emergency decrees and the Enabling Act of 1933 to destroy German democracy within months.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Fascist regimes differed: in Benito Mussolini’s Italy the party was subordinated to the state, and in <b>Adolf Hitler</b>’s Germany party and state agencies competed under a leader who stood above both.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · How It Works</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Braunau am Inn, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Führerbunker, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.26,13.03&amp;year=1925" aria-label="Braunau am Inn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4853 476 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Adolf Hitler"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5303 746h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5303 746h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.26,13.03&amp;year=1925">What was here in 1925? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
